INT. NIGHT FERRY - CAR DECK - NIGHT

Diesel. Chains rattling. Forty cars, bumper to bumper, under caged yellow lights. The ship rolls, barely.

ISOLDE VANTERPOOL, 30s, purser's vest, walks the lanes with a flashlight. She checks each windshield. Ticks a list.

She stops at a white delivery van. Taps the hood. Moves on.

RAFFERTY KANE, 50s, a trucker in a stained cap, sits on a bumper with a thermos. He raises it in greeting.

RAFFERTY
Evening, miss. Lovely crossing.

ISOLDE
Smooth water tonight.

RAFFERTY
That's what I said to my ex-wife. Look how that ended.

She almost smiles. Writes something down.

JUNG-HO PARK, 20s, headphones around his neck, leans out of a hatchback, bleary. He yawns.

JUNG-HO
Is there a vending machine on this deck?

ISOLDE
Passenger lounge. Deck four.

JUNG-HO
(to himself)
Of course there is.

He ducks back in. Isolde keeps walking.

WINNIE ASHCROFT-DELANEY, 70s, a tartan blanket over her knees, sits in a small hatchback, window cracked. A cat carrier on her lap.

WINNIE
(to the group, generally)
Does anybody know if the cats are allowed out for the crossing?

RAFFERTY
Rules say no.

WINNIE
Rules say. Hm.

Isolde leans down to her window.

ISOLDE
Ma'am, I'll check on her in an hour. Promise.

WINNIE
It's a him.

ISOLDE
I'll check on him in an hour.

She straightens. Her radio squawks.

SALAZAR (V.O.)
(over PA)
Purser to bridge. Purser to bridge. Please confirm car count.

Isolde keys her radio.

ISOLDE
Forty-one, Captain. Forty-one vehicles.

SALAZAR (V.O.)
(over PA)
Say again.

ISOLDE
Forty-one.

A long pause. Static.

SALAZAR (V.O.)
(over PA)
Manifest says forty.

She stops walking.

ISOLDE
(quietly, to herself)
Forty-one.

She turns slowly. Eyes down the lanes. Counting under her breath.

Rafferty watches her. The thermos stops halfway to his mouth.

RAFFERTY
Something wrong, love?

ISOLDE
Nothing. Routine.

She isn't convincing. She moves down the aisle, flashlight low. Stops at the white van again.

Plates: out of state. Not on her list. She hadn't ticked it because she hadn't noticed it. It had been there, quiet, like furniture.

She reaches for the rear door handle. Locked.

JUNG-HO
(from his car)
Hey, uh. That van was here when I parked.

ISOLDE
Which means?

JUNG-HO
Means nobody got out of it. Not once. I've been watching.

Rafferty sets down the thermos. Stands up. Slow.

RAFFERTY
Miss. Step back.

ISOLDE
Why?

RAFFERTY
(very calm)
Because I'm a driver. And I know what that engine sounds like when it's not an engine.

Isolde listens. Underneath the diesel and the chains, a thin electric whine. Steady. Patient.

She backs away. One step. Two.

ISOLDE
(into radio, steady)
Captain. Evacuate the car deck. All hands. Now.

SALAZAR (V.O.)
(over PA)
Purser, what is it?

Winnie rolls her window up. Clutches the carrier.

WINNIE
(to the cat, muttering)
Hush, hush, hush.

Jung-Ho is already out of the hatchback, running for the stairwell.

The van's side door slides open an inch. A single hand reaches out. Pale. Gloved. It sets a small black box on the deck.

Then the door slides shut again.

The whine climbs.

RAFFERTY
(shouting to the whole deck)
EVERYBODY OUT! MOVE!

Isolde grabs Winnie's door. Yanks it open.

ISOLDE
Ma'am. Leave the car.

WINNIE
The cat --

ISOLDE
Bring the cat.

They run. Behind them the black box blinks once. Green.

Then red.
